hi I was wondering in this in this book  we read by David Borenstein in how to  change the world he emphasizes the role  of the social entrepreneur essentially a  philanthropist who approaches social  problems in the same way she might  approach business problems and I was  hoping to gain your perspective on what  you might think about that process and  also how you might approach social  issues you know I've been a little more  in the skeptical camp on the whole  social entrepreneurship notion partly  because what I've seen is is running a  non-profit and doing things for  nonprofit is fundamentally different  than running a for-profit and you know  in spite of what I said earlier about  you know balancing constituents  constituencies I believe that for-profit  has to be a for-profit I mean quite  seriously otherwise it's not going to  survive and endure over the long run and  grow and thrive and a not-for-profit is  a very different thing and and I feel  there's been a little bit of hubris in  my community of saying oh we know better  how to run these nonprofits because  we've run these other companies so well  and some of the things translate and  some of the things don't translate and  are a very different environment and I  feel like there are some really stunning  philanthropies out there who do a  wonderful job of creating enormous value  enormous products services for people  who need it and we shouldn't be  dismissive of them as saying oh they're  not social entrepreneurs I think we  should learn from them because I think  they know a lot about how to do that  right so I feel I feel a little more  respectful of that community that I that  I hear what I hear this sort of social  entrepreneurship notion
 

